The GDPR has two fine tiers. The higher tier's maximum is €20 million or 4% of annual worldwide turnover.

**Higher tier: up to €20 million or 4%**
- The page says: "a fine up to €20 million or up to 4% of the annual worldwide turnover of the preceding financial year in case of an enterprise, whichever is greater" (Article 83, Paragraphs 5 & 6).
- The 4% figure is based on the preceding financial year and applies to an enterprise. The higher of the two amounts applies.
- It applies to infringements of these provisions:
  - the basic principles for processing, including conditions for consent (Articles 5, 6, 7 and 9);
  - the data subjects' rights (Articles 12 to 22);
  - transfers of personal data to a recipient in a third country or an international organisation (Articles 44 to 49);
  - any obligations under member state law adopted under Chapter IX;
  - noncompliance with a supervisory authority's order or a temporary or definitive limitation on processing, or suspension of data flows, under Article 58(2), or failure to provide access in violation of Article 58(1).
- The overview section hedges this with "In some cases, violators of the GDPR may be fined up to €20 million or up to 4%…".

**Lower tier: up to €10 million or 2%**
- The page says: "a fine up to €10 million or up to 2% of the annual worldwide turnover of the preceding financial year in case of an enterprise, whichever is greater" (Article 83, Paragraph 4).
- It applies to infringements of these provisions:
  - the obligations of the controller and the processor under Articles 8, 11, 25 to 39, and 42 and 43;
  - the obligations of the certification body under Articles 42 and 43;
  - the obligations of the monitoring body under Article 41(4).
- Failing to designate an EU Representative also falls in this tier. The page notes that an intentional or negligent character of that failure may be an aggravating factor.

Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Data_Protection_Regulation (sections "Remedies, liability and penalties", "Controller and processor", and "Applicability outside of the European Union > EU Representative").
